#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078269 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7768eb85-b81d-5f86-b609-5fcaeebe3028" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Increasing and Decreasing Intervals Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "a5911e0d-32a9-5c18-a653-301a5febea0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-335" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\n![](images/d5/d524aa081eec487a6107a3685f87ec97fe6895f1c3b7a91bc4bb07a637f76f40.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If $f'(x) <            0$ for all $x \\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $f'(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-5,-2),$ $(0,2)$ and $(5,\\infty)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d5/d51ade5596e25a7ba791a3090689a0eacc52e6253ea894257be7e62c71deafa4.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is decreasing when $x\\in (-5,-2) \\cup (0,2) \\cup (5,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078270 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "9427433b-9b7c-5c7a-8dce-ae40c340ce23" :question/math-academy-id "e-336" :question/problem "Let $v(t)$ denote the speed (in $\\textrm{km/h}$) of a hurricane at time $t$ (in hours) since the hurricane originated in the Atlantic Ocean. The graph of $y=v'(t),$ the derivative of $v(t),$ is given below. Find all the values of $t$ where the speed of the hurricane is decreasing. \n\n![](images/9e/9e93a8c0657797100f23b229be1a41c2c377a1711b33b4fc51fc44c8b5b03f89.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $v'(t) = 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- When $v'(t) > 0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- When $v'(t) <             0$ for all $t \\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that $v'(t)$ is negative on the intervals $[0,2)$ and $(7,8]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f4f59a5ea966ec9927caed5a870c57d63b19654330ced524998787f5837e49.png)\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hurricane is decreasing when $t \\in [0,2) \\cup (7,8].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078271 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/id #uuid "2cfa09af-7bf3-5acb-ad97-20fea53a2523" :question/math-academy-id "e-337" :question/problem "The graph of $y=z'(x),$ the derivative of $z(x),$ is given below. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y=z'(x)$ are shown in the picture.\n\n![](images/a3/a3639ee18c26d382ff8fd626f43873571fd0fffd8271228b8b1935c4d01c8212.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If $z'(x)=0$ or $z'(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If $z'(x)>0,$ then $z(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $z'(x)<         0,$ then $z(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $z(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $z'(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- $z'(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,8)$ | $8$ | $(8,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $p$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{min}$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\textrm{not extr.}$ | $\\searrow$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=3,$ $x=4,$ and $x=5.$  \n\nNotice that $x=8$ is *not* a relative extremum because the sign of $z'(x)$ does not change either side of this critical point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8c4cdf-b862-56df-9c65-900b8110f30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506708} {:answer/id #uuid "4377abc3-0473-5726-bdd3-6c9add84d178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506837} {:answer/id #uuid "72379280-e355-5f36-a105-8976f4d70c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506940} {:answer/id #uuid "acc71326-f476-55f1-aaac-75399cf7ac3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507061} {:answer/id #uuid "df531424-868c-5fc7-ba92-6d8596bfc949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72379280-e355-5f36-a105-8976f4d70c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506940} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9171a8b-863f-5a4c-98e8-4ee5f123ae53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ff0eb96-3b32-511f-94b3-5bede42b80e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114811" :question/problem "![](images/68/68ccae6ffef49cdd9704deecf4c0c33e025b807c5b5db167825387d720e554ba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $h(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $h(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to  negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${h}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $h$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain a relative maximum at $x=-4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e942993-e2db-5fce-8ad2-4a6f29461e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506709} {:answer/id #uuid "4c135e26-e278-5b1b-b485-44dd10142088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506857} {:answer/id #uuid "c7387c71-31e4-54e0-8dd3-f9acfcb11200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507111} {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1053-07ea-58e5-bc92-281881852dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507134} {:answer/id #uuid "deb4517f-3304-5627-9ed5-12be6d303e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7387c71-31e4-54e0-8dd3-f9acfcb11200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507111} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d734f7a-100d-584a-8e56-6e77f2d42946" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19ac6220-7276-52db-bb25-1688d9b81ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-114813" :question/problem "![](images/56/56a341e4982e2b91772399c81b9cef0df394320b1c640bec43ab0f39ab973143.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of a continuous function $f(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $f(x)$ has a local extremum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${r}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has a relative extremum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative extrema at $x=2$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d9f091-3ca5-5ea0-b11a-71932ccb3dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (7,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506686} {:answer/id #uuid "484d9ecf-dd71-506a-aac9-d79ca0e73403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,2)\\cup (5,8]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506846} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d6a52c-a7e3-503e-b05e-4b1160409211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,2)\\cup (4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507030} {:answer/id #uuid "e675de75-faa2-5e5b-932b-3dc45048f683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507182} {:answer/id #uuid "e69411cd-7742-53be-8fa5-1bc86cc827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (2,4)\\cup (6,8]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e69411cd-7742-53be-8fa5-1bc86cc827e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (2,4)\\cup (6,8]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507183} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e2cae9-3fc1-5f2c-aa86-c1d166158c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33cb46d7-690c-5475-8598-a16bb2a9e17a" :question/math-academy-id "q-114908" :question/problem "![](images/74/74e107d1618266bd37c4f6aa3a756b8f1fe502b55881ed058f8c0e243dbfcb75.png)\n\nLet $v(t)$ denote the speed of a car at time $t$ (in hours) since the car started its journey. The graph of $y={v}^{′}(t),$ the derivative of $v(t),$ is given above. Find all the values of $t$ where the speed of a car is decreasing." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${v}^{′}(t)=0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- When ${v}^{′}(t)>0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- When ${v}^{′}(t)<0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $v(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${v}^{′}(t)$ is negative on the intervals $(2,4)$ and $(6,8]:$ \n\n![](images/3e/3ee00947c56e4e1eecdce728d877f5dfc2d440a8d226cfa13297bc856ed928a1.png)\n\nTherefore, the speed of a car is decreasing when $t\\in (2,4)\\cup (6,8].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34721242-8f14-5bfc-8787-33d72f5d3678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506805} {:answer/id #uuid "cfdbc4b2-b1df-56d5-988f-6875717f09a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507131} {:answer/id #uuid "eba008d5-badd-5200-89fb-20e531cbf0d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507199} {:answer/id #uuid "ebcdf4d5-e860-5aa8-a71d-cd1238ef845f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=2$ and $t=7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507200} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ca3233-ced4-51ad-b9c3-2ca614be997a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34721242-8f14-5bfc-8787-33d72f5d3678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506805} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a78235e-f74a-5076-bad2-6b20392d4836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2659dfe-74f5-52bf-9b42-048773d5627d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115122" :question/problem "![](images/40/40ca6cb59eb00380e52500137521c16a1c003bcd28142f71aff41466224585e7.png)\n\nLet $f(t)$ denote the temperature of a room at time $t$ (in hours) since $0:00\\,am$ . The graph of $y={f}^{′}(t),$ the derivative of  $f(t),$ is given above. Find all the values of $t$ where the temperature of the room reached a relative minimum." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)=0$ or $f(t)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)>0,$ then $f(t)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)<0,$ then $f(t)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(t)$ has a relative minimum at $t=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(t)$ changes its sign from negative to positive (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,8)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $t=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the temperature of the room reached a relative minimum at $t=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c8c20f-e80e-5f10-854b-3285f3ad9f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,3)\\cup (6,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506714} {:answer/id #uuid "3636fd84-90c2-5a16-9c66-8f92e987c784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506808} {:answer/id #uuid "460abfae-facb-5936-9310-b258d1cf9054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=3$ and $t=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506842} {:answer/id #uuid "59ede426-9c04-58f7-a7b3-ef35e800760d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506888} {:answer/id #uuid "b615c7ef-e31b-5ce1-bf29-9e67c15d2dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (3,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c8c20f-e80e-5f10-854b-3285f3ad9f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,3)\\cup (6,9]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506714} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ddc3689-a3c8-5a72-a3ae-79690e19f16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ce903b6-3031-5567-af7e-4487037a23d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-30607" :question/problem "![](images/01/01816b967f64b920b6bc224a931fcae5aba59d5a3530dc5bac38ec79a0ee89a8.png)\n\nLet $h(t)$ denote the price of a burger at a burger outlet, where $t$ denotes the number of years since $1990.$  The graph of $y={h}^{′}(t),$ the derivative of $h(t),$ is given above. Find all the values of $t$ where the price of a burger is increasing." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(t)=0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $h(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- When ${h}^{′}(t)>0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $h(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- When ${h}^{′}(t)<0$ for all $t\\in (a,b),$ then $h(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${h}^{′}(t)$ is positive on the intervals $[0,3)$ and $(6,9]:$\n\n![](images/35/35b4ea0dc5200e0ed009b00320a88927daadd65265f245d7624edb95f57d8712.png)\n\nTherefore, the price of a burger is increasing when $t\\in [0,3)\\cup (6,9].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7768eb85-b81d-5f86-b609-5fcaeebe3028" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Increasing and Decreasing Intervals Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ead3ff-81e0-50e1-b991-0e7c7a6c681c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-4,-2)\\cup (0,2)\\cup (6,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 