First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-6)$ and $(-1,3):$ \n\n![](images/4b/4b8010c256447c91b20c0b1c3d2099d7b6739aca5c32ddb4fed357c4a3376759.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is increasing when\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-6\\,)⋃(\\,-1,3\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078406 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7768eb85-b81d-5f86-b609-5fcaeebe3028" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Increasing and Decreasing Intervals Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2be97cb-a69c-51b2-bf3c-e38ef26d56d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2be97cb-a69c-51b2-bf3c-e38ef26d56d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507173} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c4f82c-217e-5eb3-b08d-18e5263e29ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507292} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6999cc8c-186d-5d70-bb57-990951f1538c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6999cc8c-186d-5d70-bb57-990951f1538c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506928} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b9c846d-bc10-53d0-8b22-ea194fabc861" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507325} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f099f112-258d-5972-b059-1f445ae86583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f099f112-258d-5972-b059-1f445ae86583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507209} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff96b527-a19f-5ac7-9f19-2ead8bd21e8e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5b6d6d0-562f-5a33-a30e-1f1a2c36de97" :question/math-academy-id "q-344954" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c107c25b8b5c3a4ed39645c042efbd3f2f43fa127beb6d96908e0b0fb7528b37.png)\n\nThe graph of the first derivative $y={f}^{′}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=1,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $y=f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}$)⋃($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is equal to zero on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is positive on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b),$ then the slope of $f(x)$ is negative on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(1,4):$ \n\n![](images/6c/6cae9012c550041c633907b3e75b5e311b09816f3f38e1653f1fe4cdcb7e950f.png)\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is decreasing when\n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-5\\,)⋃(\\,1,4\\,).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7a4436-ea23-5859-88d7-8a7d5142ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e7a4436-ea23-5859-88d7-8a7d5142ed82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506791} :answer-field/id #uuid "1724c868-ea4e-5d8f-b2e5-934b62b813e1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507254} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe9b273a-ff4b-576e-8642-40edc9e823ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe9b273a-ff4b-576e-8642-40edc9e823ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507234} :answer-field/id #uuid "49dc86e0-3d52-5857-853a-0745b0e1ee11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d727b825-fa07-5b49-90ba-ecb0e616ecfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-344957" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f3ec85e24c748cd99a11b27e2a6a60f04bbec7edaa07ed75a108e7f3ec01b93.png)\n\nThe graph of the first derivative $y={h}^{′}(x)$ of the function $h(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-5,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=4,$ as shown above. In **ascending order**, the $x$-values where the function $h(x)$ has a relative minimum are\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $h(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $h(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $h(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=a$ if:\n\n- ${h}^{′}(a)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined at $x=a$, and\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes sign from negative to positive (left to right) at $x=a.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $-5$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${h}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $h$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $x=-5$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcba3eb-eed2-565c-868b-f2bc3be37bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dcba3eb-eed2-565c-868b-f2bc3be37bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6243e594-ca31-5fc9-b20d-7438950fe49a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0bdebd-e104-5d1f-b1a1-31221c91f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d0bdebd-e104-5d1f-b1a1-31221c91f2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506786} :answer-field/id #uuid "764b2260-d66b-5d00-87bf-9c9530b4d9b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5818398-517a-54af-aa49-c3881a2d641e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5818398-517a-54af-aa49-c3881a2d641e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507181} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e366b3-a6ff-5dab-ad42-ddf9debc4110" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35f1a1c6-9873-5841-870a-4915f1def387" :question/math-academy-id "q-344967" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5e5c9ebe4f2943aa4d3b007837a9060c6f05651a32dfecf65aab1f980ed38eb.png)\n\nLet $C(t)$ denote the concentration of a drug in the bloodstream (in milligrams per liter), where $t\\ge 0$ is the number of hours since dosing began. The graph of the first derivative $y={C}^{′}(t)$ of the function $C(t)$ intersects the $T$-axis at the points $t=3,$ $t=6,$ and $t=9,$ as shown above. Find all values of $t$ for which the concentration is increasing.\n\n$t\\in [0,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\text{ }⋃\\text{ }($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)=0$ for all $t\\in (a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)>0$ on $(a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)<0$ on $(a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${C}^{′}(t)$ is positive on the intervals $[0,3)$ and $(6,9):$\n\n![](images/58/586fd368d9693df248c212465d9d3a43c6355dc56b224e3ba22d84950fd05f96.png)\n\nTherefore, the concentration is increasing when $t\\in [0,\\,3\\,)\\text{ }⋃\\text{ }(\\,6,\\,9\\,).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078410 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c78f5b5f-45e2-543a-b4b1-ca5a558b6f86" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Graph of a Function's Derivative: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d03eb08-241f-53ed-b297-cb67638980c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506901}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d03eb08-241f-53ed-b297-cb67638980c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c77da8e1-44c9-5c56-a9c9-8ce6ba09a2e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "265d6660-f3f4-589d-8c58-624bba5f0050" :question/math-academy-id "q-344987" :question/problem "![](images/40/407f8bae5534f4d5274a22de084d8bb643f5ae2f27d2a0634c85a35689d91fcd.png)\n\nLet $f(t)$ denote the altitude of a drone during a flight test at time $t$ (in meters), where $t$ (in minutes) is the time after the test begins. The graph of $y={f}^{′}(t),$ the derivative of $f(t),$ is given above. Find the value of $t$ where the altitude of the drone reached a relative minimum.\n\n$t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(t)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)>0,$ then $f(t)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(t)<0,$ then $f(t)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(t)$ has a relative minimum at $t=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(a)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(t)$ changes its sign from negative to positive (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,9)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $t=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the altitude of THE drone reached a relative minimum at $t=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d407416-d337-55ef-aac1-8cb2dbf8923a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506735} {:answer/id #uuid "5be2f471-f248-5b50-8c7a-0bb2807aa110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3.5,x=1.5,$ and $x=5.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506897} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5e6516-a46e-571e-b248-d0070f7b62ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506932} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e1895e-4182-5f97-8f86-bb122a26710d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3.5$ and $x=5.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507033} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc1e4da-8b59-5e7c-945b-4265b9d09e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d407416-d337-55ef-aac1-8cb2dbf8923a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506735} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ccd75a2-bfd3-5ab2-b1b6-512c8c143b31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a82a5e57-2cf5-51c6-8038-f4b6b4ab4954" :question/math-academy-id "q-5214" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd080f939b3ac98c50edb6ae92a88a2aaed11dc54f0b3a28eb561b34d6f1915a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={f}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${f}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $-5$ | $(-5,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,6)$ | $6$ | $(6,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative maxima at $x=-5$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078414 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c1bec57-410f-53cc-a3bd-f48167777932" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Relative Extrema Given the Graph of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1be8cab2-8cbf-54f7-af5e-388a4bccb94d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506729} {:answer/id #uuid "42beb581-6dcf-520c-b64c-bbf621cd971f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506835} {:answer/id #uuid "48eda7a8-5f9f-5698-a10a-d628d82c938e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506849} {:answer/id #uuid "536b40ad-5880-5469-8d2c-1b4ac8dcb91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$, $x=-1$, and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506869} {:answer/id #uuid "54148e00-41c9-5b1f-a637-9cf32216ab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54148e00-41c9-5b1f-a637-9cf32216ab8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506872} :answer-field/id #uuid "032aeb99-4d97-54c5-8fa6-ed5f567b7ff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b070ee31-f187-53ba-a781-62e45a87b27d" :question/math-academy-id "q-80079" :question/problem "![](images/90/909d6bd6527723d430f66a947b1cdbb78afc2d902f15607faf94779144dbdae3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $h(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $h(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${h}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $h$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain relative minima at $x=-3$ and $x=4$ only."}]}
